\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_feature_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across depths $d{=}0$ to $d{=}3$, stratified by model (columns) and strategy (line style). Top row: \texttt{p90\_ppl} (monotonically decreasing). Middle: \texttt{var\_surprisal} (decreasing). Bottom: \texttt{rep\_3gram} (increasing). Shaded bands: interquartile range. The $d{=}0 \to d{=}1$ gap exceeds all subsequent inter-depth gaps.}
\label{fig:feature_trajectory}
\end{figure}

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{EDD Analysis}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

We extend the benchmark to depth $d{=}5$ for all three models under nucleus sampling.
Each model's depth-3 LoRA checkpoint is used to finetune and generate depth-4 data, and the process repeats for depth~5; feature extraction uses the same reference model (GPT-2 base, 124M).
We train dedicated binary RF classifiers (500 trees) for each adjacent pair, as defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}.

\paragraph{AUC decay profile.}
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} plots the binary pairwise AUC decay curves.
For Pythia-1.4B (nucleus), AUC decays monotonically: d0-vs-d1 $= 0.999$, d1-vs-d2 $= 0.925$, d2-vs-d3 $= 0.823$, d3-vs-d4 $= 0.784$, d4-vs-d5 $= 0.683$.
The steepest drop occurs between the d1-vs-d2 and d2-vs-d3 pairs; the curve then flattens as the system approaches an indistinguishable regime gradually rather than crossing a sharp boundary.

\paragraph{Cross-model convergence at depth 4--5.}
The d3-vs-d4 AUC clusters near $T{=}0.75$ (Pythia $0.784$, OLMo $0.765$, GPT-2~XL $0.750$; bootstrap CIs in Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay}), while d4-vs-d5 falls uniformly below 0.70 ($0.681$--$0.684$), confirming model-independent signal saturation at depth~5.
This graded decay supports interpreting the d3--d4 region as a \emph{transition zone} rather than a binary boundary.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} reports $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ across the primary 9-cell benchmark (depths 0--3) at four threshold values.
At $T{=}0.65$, all 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD} = 3$; at $T{=}0.75$, 7 of 9 cells maintain $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$; at $T{=}0.80$, the count drops to 5 of 9.
The cells losing $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ first are consistently the top-$k$ configurations (Pythia top-$k$ at $T{=}0.70$, then OLMo top-$k$ at $T{=}0.75$), where the d2-vs-d3 pair is the consistent bottleneck across all 9 cells.

